>h1 Cupid toolchain workers

Mount the compiler source ISO at /src. Run the staged worker ELF with exec.

>h2 Compile a source

Place two LF-terminated lines in /home/cupidc.job. The first names the
source relative to the ISO root. The second names the output under /home.
An input under /home is read from HomeFS. Put local quoted headers next
to that source.

  /toolchain/ctool.cc
  /home/ctool.o

Add option lines after the output path to select a source's build profile.
Use include for quoted and angle headers, angle for angle headers alone,
define NAME=VALUE for macros, and force for an absolute header path.
Use doom on its own line for the existing Doom compatibility rules.
When a job has options, list all include roots in search order.
The compiler reads all source and header paths from the same filesystem.
Use up to 64 search roots, 16 forced headers and 64 definitions.

To compile several sources with the same profile, use a list path first,
a /home output ending in .iso second and bundle third. Put any profile
options below bundle. Each list entry is a source path under the /src mount
followed by a root-level ISO member path ending in .o. Use one path per line.

>h2 Link objects

Place the output, text base, entry symbol and object paths in
/home/cupidld.job. Put one field or path on each line. Object order
matches the link order.

  /home/hello.elf
  0x01c00000
  _start
  /home/hello.o

Use 0x01c00000 for an ordinary executable and 0x01e00000 for a compiler
worker. The standalone compiler uses cupidc_guest_run as its entry.
Each worker returns to the shell after reporting its result.
Wait for its lease to be released before starting another worker.
Both workers release cached work buffers before publishing output and
again when the job ends.

For a script layout, write script on the second line and the absolute
script path on the third line. List objects in link order below it.

  /home/kernel.elf
  script
  /disk/link.ld
  /disk/obj0/i000.o

>h2 Generate kernel symbols

In /home/cupidksyms.job, put the pass-one ELF path and the output source
path on separate lines.

  /home/pass1.elf
  /home/ksyms_data.cc

Stage the symbol worker as /cupks and run exec /disk/cupks. Compile the
generated source with CupidC and append its object to the second link.
Increasing initializer indices need one duplicate check per validation stage.

>h2 Run a build plan

Stage cupidbuild-guest.elf as /b. Use cd to enter your project directory,
then run exec /disk/b. Write each operation and its job path on separate
lines in the project's cupidbuild.job.

  compile
  jobs/hello.cj
  link
  jobs/hello.lj

Job paths in the project plan may also be absolute. If the directory has
no plan, CupidBuild uses /home/cupidbuild.job with absolute job paths.

Each step uses the format of its standalone job. Use raw for checked boot
assembly, symbols for kernel symbols, object for wrapping or flattening,
assemble for an ELF object, install for generated kernel source, and disk
for a bootable disk prefix. The driver validates up to 512 steps first
and stops at the first failed step. Each step publishes output atomically.
Use /tmp for intermediate files and /home for outputs that need to survive
a reboot.

>h2 Compile or wrap several objects

A compile-list job starts with the usual compiler options, then a line
containing sources. Write one source path and one output .o path per
pair. CupidBuild uses the same options for every source.
An optional first line such as root /home/project selects the source
directory. Source and include paths keep their names within the project.

An asset-list job starts with wrap, wrap-text or wrap-jpeg, followed by
the absolute asset directory. Each source path is relative to that
directory and starts with a slash. The worker uses it to name the ELF
symbols. The next line names the output .o file.

Each list accepts up to 256 pairs and requires distinct output paths.
If a worker fails, earlier published products remain available.

>h2 Run a plan from a read-only ISO

Build cupidbuild-media.elf with make -C bootstrap/guest guest-media-build.
Stage it as /b, mount the plan ISO at /plan, and run exec /disk/b.
The media entry reads /plan/cupidbuild.job. Each job path must start with
/plan/jobs/. The source replay reads headers and build inputs from source
ISOs and starts with a fresh HomeFS for outputs.
The production plan ISO also carries assets under /plan/assets.

Use kernel followed by the job path to build the bootloader, both kernel
ELFs and the disk prefix. The job lists up to 511 distinct pass-one .o
paths in link order, one per line. CupidBuild reads boot/boot.asm and
link.ld from /src, generates the symbol object between links, and saves
the disk prefix as /home/disk-prefix.img.
The editable plan can also run this job. Add root /home/project on its
first line to read boot/boot.asm, link.ld and headers from that tree.
Add output /home/project/build after root and before the object list to
put all seven kernel products there. Create the directory first. Without
an output line, the job writes to /home.

>h2 Assemble a relocatable object

  as -f elf32 -o /home/start.o /home/start.asm

The assembler gives .rodata four-byte default alignment. CupidLD places
the bytes in a read-only ELF segment and resolves references to its labels.

For a production kernel object, run the standalone assembler worker. Put
the assembly source and the /home object path in /home/cupidasm.job.

  /disk/isr.asm
  /home/isr.o

The worker keeps symbols case-sensitive and uses the source's own names.
CupidDis checks instruction bytes, local jumps and function starts before
the worker replaces the saved object.

>h2 Compile peer tool sources

Use the same compiler job format with /toolchain/cupidasm.cc,
/toolchain/cupiddis.cc or /toolchain/cupidobj.cc. The ISO includes their
headers. Keep the resulting objects under /home.

>h2 Build an example user program

When the source ISO includes /user/examples and /user/cupid.h, compile
/user/examples/hello.cc to /home/hello.o with include /user. Link it at
0x01c00000 with entry _start. Run the result with exec /home/hello.elf.

>h2 Flatten an ELF image

The CupidObj worker reads /home/cupidobj.job. Put the input ELF path on the
first line and a /home output path on the second line.

  /disk/kern.elf
  /home/kernel.bin

Stage the worker as /cupobj on the disk and run exec /disk/cupobj.
HomeFS saves the worker output through a replacement file. FAT16 syncs its
data chain before publishing the updated directory entry.

>h2 Wrap a kernel asset

Use the same request file. After the input and output paths, add wrap for
binary data or wrap-text for source text, then the path from the repository
root. That last path supplies the ELF symbol names.

  /disk/text.src
  /home/text.o
  wrap-text
  /bin/ls.cc

For read-only data, use wrap-readonly and put the ELF symbol stem on the
last line. Use smp_trampoline for the AP startup image.

Use wrap-jpeg for sequential JPEG images. The worker checks the stream
and verifies the object payload and identity symbols before publication.

Use wrap-bundle on the third line to wrap several assets into one ISO.
The first line then names a list file and the second names the output ISO.
Each list entry gives the asset path, the ISO member path, the wrapping
mode and the repository path or symbol stem. Use one field per line.

>h2 Generate kernel installation source

In /home/cupidinstall.job, put the output source path, the installation
kind and the input groups. Use one repository path per line.

  /home/demos_programs_gen.cc
  demos
  --demos
  /demos/hello.asm

For bin, use --bin, --headers and --browser in that order. For docs, use
--ctxt, --doc-assets and --home-assets. Keep the heading for an empty group.
Stage the installation worker as /cupinst and run exec /disk/cupinst.

>h2 Assemble a checked boot image

Stage the raw assembly worker as /cupraw. In /home/cupidraw.job, put the
source path, output path and bootloader or smp-trampoline on separate lines.

  /disk/boot.asm
  /home/boot.bin
  bootloader

Run exec /disk/cupraw. CupidDis checks the instructions and branch targets
before the worker publishes the raw image. The trampoline also has to match
the kernel's startup layout.

>h2 Build a disk template

Assemble the bootloader and check its saved range map.

  as -f bin --map /home/boot.map -o /home/boot.bin /disk/boot.asm
  dis --raw --range-map /home/boot.map --require-known /home/boot.bin

In /home/cupiddisk.job, put the five-sector bootloader path, raw kernel
path and output path on separate lines.

  /home/boot.bin
  /home/kernel.bin
  /home/disk-prefix.img

Stage the worker as /cupdisk and run exec /disk/cupdisk. The result ends
after the empty FAT16 root directory. It describes the standard 200 MiB
image with its partition starting at sector 20480.
